WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:01.968 line:0%
-Our next guest is one of the

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:03.403 line:0%
best-selling authors
of all time.

00:00:03.403 --> 00:00:06.072 line:0%
His autobiography,
"James Patterson
by James Patterson:

00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:07.907 line:0%
The Stories of My Life"
is on sale now.

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:09.975 line:0%
Please welcome back to the show
James Patterson.

00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:12.245
[ Cheering and applause ]

00:00:12.245 --> 00:00:21.387 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.387 --> 00:00:28.227 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:28.227 --> 00:00:29.662
Welcome back to the show!

00:00:29.662 --> 00:00:31.664
-This is so cool.
I'm not usually up this late.

00:00:31.664 --> 00:00:33.833
[ Laughter ]
Yeah, it's a big deal
to have you

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:35.234
past the witching hour.

00:00:35.234 --> 00:00:37.704
So, last time you were here
was with a book you co-wrote

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:39.772
with former
President Bill Clinton.

00:00:39.772 --> 00:00:41.574
-Oh, yeah, I forgot about that.

00:00:41.574 --> 00:00:42.675
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:43.910
You've written books
with Dolly Parton.

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.945
Is it nice to be out with the

00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:47.446
-By myself, yeah, yeah, yeah.
-spotlight all to yourself?

00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:49.582
-No, this is good. This is good.
I like it. I like it.

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:51.684
-You, obviously,
you write thrillers.

00:00:51.684 --> 00:00:53.019
You come up
with thrilling titles,

00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:54.387
the kind of title
that make people say,

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:56.355
"I've got to read this book."
-Are you suggesting that

00:00:56.355 --> 00:00:58.958
that title isn't any good?
[ Laughter ]

00:00:58.958 --> 00:01:01.427 line:0%
It's clear.
-"James Patterson
by James Patterson"?

00:01:01.427 --> 00:01:02.829 line:0%
[ Laughter ]
-Here's a question I have
for you, though --

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:03.930 line:0%
who the hell's the guy
on the cover?

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:05.164
-I was going to say
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:05.164 --> 00:01:06.799
because I went and said hi
to you backstage

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:08.034
and then I saw this book
and I'm like,

00:01:08.034 --> 00:01:08.935
"What's going on here?"

00:01:08.935 --> 00:01:10.636
[ Laughter ]

00:01:10.636 --> 00:01:12.238
-That's exactly what happens
to me.

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:14.173
I go to the mirror --
I go to the --

00:01:14.173 --> 00:01:16.109
get up in the morning,
stumble into the bathroom,

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:17.477
and I look in the mirror
and I go,

00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:18.545
"Oh, you again."

00:01:18.545 --> 00:01:20.679
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.679 --> 00:01:22.849
-You talk about your, you know,
journey as a writer.

00:01:22.849 --> 00:01:24.717
Obviously,
you could never have guessed

00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:27.019
you would sell
over 400 million books

00:01:27.019 --> 00:01:28.221
over the course of your career.

00:01:28.221 --> 00:01:30.590
-Or 4,000.
-Any, yeah,

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:33.226
any number is a big number.
But you go to a bookstore early

00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:36.428
in your career, when you're
about to find out your first --

00:01:36.428 --> 00:01:37.763
you're going to have
your first hit book.

00:01:37.763 --> 00:01:39.999
-Yeah.
-And a pretty interesting thing

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:42.568
happens when you see somebody
looking at it.

00:01:42.568 --> 00:01:45.438
-Well, yeah, it was
on The Times Best-Seller List,

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:47.973
but I didn't believe it, so I
went into a local bookstore.

00:01:47.973 --> 00:01:50.410
And what'll happen
with writers is,

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:53.145
if you pick up the book
and we're in the store,

00:01:53.145 --> 00:01:54.647
we're watching you.

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:56.348
If you put it down,
it breaks our heart.

00:01:56.348 --> 00:01:57.617
Not kidding.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.617 --> 00:02:00.553
If you buy it, it makes our day.

00:02:00.553 --> 00:02:03.355
So I'm watching this woman --
this is early in my career --

00:02:03.355 --> 00:02:06.058
and she looks at it
and she looks at the back.

00:02:06.058 --> 00:02:07.727
And then she walks
down the aisle and I said,

00:02:07.727 --> 00:02:10.363
"This is the best. I want to go
hug this woman," you know?

00:02:10.363 --> 00:02:12.231
She puts it on her bag.
She stole it.

00:02:12.231 --> 00:02:14.600
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:17.469
And all I'm going is,
"Does that count as a sale?"

00:02:17.469 --> 00:02:20.540
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:02:20.540 --> 00:02:22.375
Do you ever introduce yourself
to someone?

00:02:22.375 --> 00:02:23.609
I mean,
you must see people reading

00:02:23.609 --> 00:02:25.611
a James Patterson.
-I did, except, years ago,

00:02:25.611 --> 00:02:27.947
I introduced myself
at a swimming pool to this guy.

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:29.081
I said, "How are you liking it?"

00:02:29.081 --> 00:02:29.949
He goes, "It's [bleep]."

00:02:29.949 --> 00:02:31.851
[ Laughter ]

00:02:31.851 --> 00:02:34.654
That's -- It's terrible.
So I don't do it anymore.

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:36.689
[ Laughter ]
That was the last time.

00:02:36.689 --> 00:02:38.424
You learn your lesson
when you get hit like that.

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:41.260
-Do you get recognized
as yourself now?

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:43.062
Certainly, this cover photo's
not going to help.

00:02:43.062 --> 00:02:45.064
[ Laughter ]
-No, I know.

00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:46.366
That's my hope.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:48.501
No, just last week,
my wife's out of town.

00:02:48.501 --> 00:02:49.869
I go to this little
Italian restaurant

00:02:49.869 --> 00:02:52.171
that we really like
and they say,

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:53.573
"Well, we'll fit you in."

00:02:53.573 --> 00:02:55.742
So the waiter takes me
down a little aisle.

00:02:55.742 --> 00:02:57.744
This woman pops up.
This is a true story.

00:02:57.744 --> 00:02:59.011
Tragically.

00:02:59.011 --> 00:03:01.714
[ Laughter ]
And she goes, "I know you.

00:03:01.714 --> 00:03:03.349
You sold us
our insurance policy."

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:05.718
[ Laughter ]
This is true.

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:09.756
And then I go to my table
and I'm sitting there

00:03:09.756 --> 00:03:12.891
and I get my appetizer
and the guy behind goes,

00:03:12.891 --> 00:03:14.527
"Are you from Boston?"

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:15.694
And I turn around.
He goes,

00:03:15.694 --> 00:03:16.863
"You're Tom Clancy!"

00:03:16.863 --> 00:03:18.598
[ Laughter ]

00:03:18.598 --> 00:03:20.332
I go, "No."

00:03:20.332 --> 00:03:21.934
If I am,
we're all in big trouble

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:24.904
because he died nine years ago
and it's a horror movie.

00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:27.206
[ Laughter ]

00:03:27.206 --> 00:03:29.242
-You know, you write --
Your books are sort of known

00:03:29.242 --> 00:03:32.511
for being very gripping --
short chapters.

00:03:32.511 --> 00:03:34.179
-Like this one, gripping.
-Well, it is, though.

00:03:34.179 --> 00:03:36.382
I mean, this reads --

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:39.185
this is so readable
and it has this, you know,

00:03:39.185 --> 00:03:42.121
momentum within it that you
expect from one of your books.

00:03:42.121 --> 00:03:45.058
Did you sit down to write it
like a thriller?

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:48.160
-You know, it was COVID and I,
you know,

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:50.095
we're thinking about life
and whatever and I just said,

00:03:50.095 --> 00:03:52.798
"Let me just start writing down
some stories."

00:03:52.798 --> 00:03:54.100
And I'm always conscious,

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.768
but I always try to pretend
there's somebody sitting

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:57.837
across from me,
I'm telling them a story,

00:03:57.837 --> 00:03:59.706
I don't want them to get up
until I'm finished.

00:03:59.706 --> 00:04:01.140
You could get up now.
It'd be funny.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:04.243
[ Laughter ]

00:04:04.243 --> 00:04:07.880
And that's kind of what I --
I mean, that's my, my deal.

00:04:07.880 --> 00:04:09.314
-We were talking backstage.
I said I heard you speak

00:04:09.314 --> 00:04:12.318
at an award show where you were
being honored and you --

00:04:12.318 --> 00:04:14.186
-You sent me the nicest note.

00:04:14.186 --> 00:04:17.357
-Well, you were very funny
and in a room where

00:04:17.357 --> 00:04:18.891
-But that was then.
-Yeah, that was then.

00:04:18.891 --> 00:04:20.259
[ Laughter ]

00:04:20.259 --> 00:04:23.028
You also told a beautiful story
about your grandfather

00:04:23.028 --> 00:04:25.931
and the way -- a thing
he told you about work.

00:04:25.931 --> 00:04:27.099
-Yeah, well, he used to --

00:04:27.099 --> 00:04:28.400
I'm a little kid
up in Newburgh, New York,

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.369
and it was a really
great thing for me.

00:04:30.369 --> 00:04:31.704
I'm eight, nine, ten years old.

00:04:31.704 --> 00:04:34.373
He would take me
on his delivery route

00:04:34.373 --> 00:04:37.009
and he was a great guy

00:04:37.009 --> 00:04:39.378
and it would go
over the Storm King Mountain

00:04:39.378 --> 00:04:40.979
and he'd be singing
in a terrible voice.

00:04:40.979 --> 00:04:43.616
He'd sing all these old songs,
"Oh, Susannah," whatever.

00:04:43.616 --> 00:04:46.852
And he said, he said, "You know,
the secret is," he says,

00:04:46.852 --> 00:04:49.221
"I hope, when you grow up,

00:04:49.221 --> 00:04:50.589
that, when you go
over the mountain

00:04:50.589 --> 00:04:52.858
to work in the morning,
you'll be singing."

00:04:52.858 --> 00:04:54.727
And I do.
I just hope other people do.

00:04:54.727 --> 00:04:56.129
I don't --
I don't work for a living.

00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:58.364
I play for a living.
I suspect you do, too.

00:04:58.364 --> 00:04:59.599
-I very much feel that way.
-Except when you get

00:04:59.599 --> 00:05:01.734
a bad guest, and then --
[ Laughter ]

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:04.737
-You keep a basket of ideas
on your desk.

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:06.138
These are ideas for books?

00:05:06.138 --> 00:05:08.341
-A very clever thing on a tile.
It says "Ideas," yeah.

00:05:08.341 --> 00:05:09.508
[ Laughter ]

00:05:09.508 --> 00:05:11.043
-And do you just sort of
randomly reach in

00:05:11.043 --> 00:05:12.611
when you feel like it's time?
-No, I don't randomly.

00:05:12.611 --> 00:05:14.280
I'll just start going
through them and I'll go,

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:16.215
"What about, what about?"
And it's partly that

00:05:16.215 --> 00:05:17.983
I feel excited about --

00:05:17.983 --> 00:05:20.186
Sometimes you'll put something
away and, two years later,

00:05:20.186 --> 00:05:22.855
you look at it and you go,
"I know where it goes now."

00:05:22.855 --> 00:05:23.922
I didn't -- I didn't know --

00:05:23.922 --> 00:05:25.358
I didn't know
where it was going.

00:05:25.358 --> 00:05:27.493
-Do you still get excited,
having written all these books,

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:29.061
to see an idea for the first
and you go,

00:05:29.061 --> 00:05:30.629
[ Snapping fingers ]
"Oh, now I know"?
-Yeah. Oh, yeah.

00:05:30.629 --> 00:05:32.298
No, no, every day is --
Not every day,

00:05:32.298 --> 00:05:34.600
but a lot of time --
Well, you know how it works.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:36.336
I mean, just something.
Yeah, it's clicking.

00:05:36.336 --> 00:05:38.036
"This is work.
I can make this work."

00:05:38.036 --> 00:05:40.005
Or "this chapter work."
-I think it's exciting

00:05:40.005 --> 00:05:41.907
because you just never know
when it's going to happen again.

00:05:41.907 --> 00:05:43.375
-Yeah, well, there's that.
-Yeah.

00:05:43.375 --> 00:05:45.711
[ Laughter ]
-We try not to think
about that too much.

00:05:45.711 --> 00:05:46.946
-Absolutely.
-See, like with this thing,

00:05:46.946 --> 00:05:49.214
I signed up
with Little Brown, so --

00:05:49.214 --> 00:05:52.452
with the autobiography --
and I'm writing another one

00:05:52.452 --> 00:05:54.686
when I'm 90
and another one when I'm 110.

00:05:54.686 --> 00:05:55.921
-Oh, that's very smart.
-Yeah.

00:05:55.921 --> 00:05:56.989
-You get that three-book deal.

00:05:56.989 --> 00:05:58.224
-And I got the money upfront.

00:05:58.224 --> 00:06:00.392
[ Laughter ]
-Thank you for being here.

00:06:00.392 --> 00:06:01.561
Congrats on the book.
-Thank you.

00:06:01.561 --> 00:06:04.229
-Such a pleasure.
James Patterson, everybody.

00:06:04.229 --> 00:06:06.132
[ Cheering and applause ]
"James Patterson
by James Patterson"

00:06:06.132 --> 00:06:08.534
is on sale now. We'll be right
back with more "Late Night."

